\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces (blue) uncertainty (standard error) in the inferred Pb--Pb age of a sample with true age $t$, due to the uncertainty in the decay constants of ${}^{238}{\rm  U}$ and especially ${}^{235}{\rm  U}$; (red) uncertainty in the difference between the inferred Pb--Pb age of a sample with true age $t$, and a second sample with age $t_{\rm  SS}$, Pb--Pb dated using identical decay constants which are uncertain. When used as a relative chronometer to date differences in formation times between two objects, the uncertainty is small (roughly 1\% of the age difference); but when used as an absolute chronometer, the $2\,\sigma $ uncertainty is about 9 Myr.}}{3}{figure.caption.1}\protected@file@percent }
\providecommand*\caption@xref[2]{\@setref\relax\@undefined{#1}}
\newlabel{fig:lambda_err}{{1}{3}{(blue) uncertainty (standard error) in the inferred Pb--Pb age of a sample with true age $t$, due to the uncertainty in the decay constants of ${}^{238}{\rm U}$ and especially ${}^{235}{\rm U}$; (red) uncertainty in the difference between the inferred Pb--Pb age of a sample with true age $t$, and a second sample with age $t_{\rm SS}$, Pb--Pb dated using identical decay constants which are uncertain. When used as a relative chronometer to date differences in formation times between two objects, the uncertainty is small (roughly 1\% of the age difference); but when used as an absolute chronometer, the $2\,\sigma $ uncertainty is about 9 Myr}{figure.caption.1}{}}
